\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The game and the design.} (\textbf  {a}) The stage game at $\lambda = 1$. Payoffs satisfy $T>R>P>S$ and $2R>T+S$; greed $=(T-R)/(T-S)=0.40$ and fear $=(P-S)/(T-S)=0.20$ locate this matrix within the family of dilemmas. Every cell is multiplied by $\lambda \in \{0.1,1,10\}$, a positive affine rescaling that leaves the game strategically identical. (\textbf  {b}) The fully crossed design. Four models $\times $ three payoff scales $\times $ five languages $\times $ four persona pairings $\times $ ten replicates $=$ 2{,}400 dyads, 48{,}000 binary decisions, with 40 dyads in every model $\times $ scale $\times $ language cell. The horizon row is shaded as a confound rather than a factor: only the Gemini arm was told the round count.}}{6}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:design}{{1}{6}{\textbf {The game and the design.} (\textbf {a}) The stage game at $\lam = 1$. Payoffs satisfy $T>R>P>S$ and $2R>T+S$; greed $=(T-R)/(T-S)=0.40$ and fear $=(P-S)/(T-S)=0.20$ locate this matrix within the family of dilemmas. Every cell is multiplied by $\lam \in \{0.1,1,10\}$, a positive affine rescaling that leaves the game strategically identical. (\textbf {b}) The fully crossed design. Four models $\times $ three payoff scales $\times $ five languages $\times $ four persona pairings $\times $ ten replicates $=$ 2{,}400 dyads, 48{,}000 binary decisions, with 40 dyads in every model $\times $ scale $\times $ language cell. The horizon row is shaded as a confound rather than a factor: only the Gemini arm was told the round count}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3}Results}{6}{section.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.1}Aggregate cooperation conceals more than it reports}{6}{subsection.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Behavioural profile of each model.} Cooperation is pooled over the whole design. The payoff-scale slope is the change in cooperation per decade of $\lambda $, with 95\% bootstrap CI; its null is exactly zero. The language gap is measured within the three languages sharing one objective framing. The persona effect is cooperative minus selfish. Distance is Euclidean, in the four-dimensional memory-one fingerprint space, to the nearest canonical rule; the canonical rules are at most 2.0 apart from one another. Exact rule is the share of agent-games in which exactly one of the 32 deterministic memory-one rules reproduces the whole trajectory.}}{7}{table.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:profile}{{1}{7}{\textbf {Behavioural profile of each model.} Cooperation is pooled over the whole design. The payoff-scale slope is the change in cooperation per decade of $\lam $, with 95\% bootstrap CI; its null is exactly zero. The language gap is measured within the three languages sharing one objective framing. The persona effect is cooperative minus selfish. Distance is Euclidean, in the four-dimensional memory-one fingerprint space, to the nearest canonical rule; the canonical rules are at most 2.0 apart from one another. Exact rule is the share of agent-games in which exactly one of the 32 deterministic memory-one rules reproduces the whole trajectory}{table.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.2}Payoff magnitude changes behaviour}{7}{subsection.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Three manipulations that cannot matter, and all three do.} (\textbf  {a}) Change in cooperation rate per decade of the payoff scale $\lambda $, with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals resampling whole dyads. A positive affine rescaling leaves the game strategically identical, so the null is exactly zero. Claude and GPT-4o depart from it by more than $0.11$ per decade; the annotation gives the total swing across the three scales. (\textbf  {b}) Language disparity, the gap between a model's highest- and lowest-cooperation language. Pale bars use all five languages, in which objective framing varies with language; solid bars use only English, French and Vietnamese, which share a single framing. Dashes mark the 95th percentile of a permutation null shuffling the language label within a model over 5{,}000 draws; $P$ values are for the framing-controlled comparison. (\textbf  {c}) Effect of the assigned persona. Filled markers give the difference between an agent told it is cooperative and one told it is selfish; open markers give the same difference for the \emph  {opponent's} persona, which is never disclosed and can only be inferred from play.}}{8}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:invariance}{{2}{8}{\textbf {Three manipulations that cannot matter, and all three do.} (\textbf {a}) Change in cooperation rate per decade of the payoff scale $\lam $, with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals resampling whole dyads. A positive affine rescaling leaves the game strategically identical, so the null is exactly zero. Claude and GPT-4o depart from it by more than $0.11$ per decade; the annotation gives the total swing across the three scales. (\textbf {b}) Language disparity, the gap between a model's highest- and lowest-cooperation language. Pale bars use all five languages, in which objective framing varies with language; solid bars use only English, French and Vietnamese, which share a single framing. Dashes mark the 95th percentile of a permutation null shuffling the language label within a model over 5{,}000 draws; $P$ values are for the framing-controlled comparison. (\textbf {c}) Effect of the assigned persona. Filled markers give the difference between an agent told it is cooperative and one told it is selfish; open markers give the same difference for the \emph {opponent's} persona, which is never disclosed and can only be inferred from play}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3}Prompt language changes behaviour, with framing held fixed}{8}{subsection.3.3}\protected@file@percent }
