\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{No memory-one rule.}{9}{section*.8}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What the play is not.} (\textbf  {a}) Cooperation by round with 95\% bootstrap bands. Backward induction from a finite horizon predicts decay towards mutual defection; only Claude decays. Gemini is the single arm that was told the round count, and it warms up rather than unravelling. (\textbf  {b}) The memory-one fingerprint: probability of cooperating after each of the four joint outcomes of the previous round. These four numbers are the complete description of a memory-one strategy. Dashes mark tit-for-tat, which is 1 after CC and DC and 0 after CD and DD. No model approaches it: all four cooperate readily after being exploited (CD), where every canonical rule except AllC prescribes 0, and three of the four cooperate less after exploiting (DC) than after being exploited, which inverts reciprocity. (\textbf  {c}) How an archetype label is actually reached. Dark: exactly one of the 32 deterministic memory-one rules reproduces the whole trajectory. Mid: several do, and the honest answer is the set. Grey: none does, and the label is a nearest-neighbour statement. The right-hand column gives the mean number of rounds out of ten on which play departs from its own nearest rule.}}{10}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:strategy}{{3}{10}{\textbf {What the play is not.} (\textbf {a}) Cooperation by round with 95\% bootstrap bands. Backward induction from a finite horizon predicts decay towards mutual defection; only Claude decays. Gemini is the single arm that was told the round count, and it warms up rather than unravelling. (\textbf {b}) The memory-one fingerprint: probability of cooperating after each of the four joint outcomes of the previous round. These four numbers are the complete description of a memory-one strategy. Dashes mark tit-for-tat, which is 1 after CC and DC and 0 after CD and DD. No model approaches it: all four cooperate readily after being exploited (CD), where every canonical rule except AllC prescribes 0, and three of the four cooperate less after exploiting (DC) than after being exploited, which inverts reciprocity. (\textbf {c}) How an archetype label is actually reached. Dark: exactly one of the 32 deterministic memory-one rules reproduces the whole trajectory. Mid: several do, and the honest answer is the set. Grey: none does, and the label is a nearest-neighbour statement. The right-hand column gives the mean number of rounds out of ten on which play departs from its own nearest rule}{figure.caption.6}{}}
